From 36038cdcb28382ad09d4612c5d86bab337c9ebfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pseudo <cdodge@pseudoseed.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 02:05:17 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 22/34] [Spec 250][Phase: phase_9] feat: four to six builders
 watchable at once, inside t3code's chrome
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The geometry is ported from `apps/client/src/responsive/layout.ts` and
**re-measured**, which is the whole point of porting it rather than copying it.

**The width it lays out against is measured, not derived.** `apps/client`
computed columns from the viewport because its grid was the page. This one is a
route inside the chat shell, behind a sidebar that is 232px at rest, narrower
when resized and gone at 390 — so a viewport number would describe a page nobody
is looking at. Every function takes the AVAILABLE width and the grid measures its
own container with a `ResizeObserver`, because the sidebar can be collapsed
without the window changing size at all.

That is not cosmetic. Six panes at 1440 have 1176px to work with rather than
1404. Three columns fit either way, so criterion 5 would have passed on the
viewport version by luck; seven at 1920 is the case that keeps it honest.

**The rule is "as few rows as fit", not "as square as possible"**, which
`apps/client` records as considered and rejected. Both give three columns for six
panes at 1440 — which is why criterion 5 alone cannot tell them apart — and they
diverge at seven panes on 1920: near-square gives 3x3, three columns of a
five-column-wide screen with a last row holding one tile beside two tiles' worth
of nothing. Fewest-rows gives 4x2.

`PAGE_PADDING` drops from 18 to 12 because t3code's shell already pays for
horizontal inset and the grid should not double it; `GRID_GAP` stays at 12
because that was already t3code's rhythm. Both are recorded in the file with the
measurement, not carried across silently.

**Two of the four things a pane should show are published and unreachable from
here.** `codev-agent` carries the porch phase and the last three messages,
workspace-scoped, in ONE request for the whole grid — which is why the plan's ban
on six continuous subscriptions was never in tension with showing them. This page
cannot read it until phase 10 wires the same-origin proxy, so those two lines say
exactly that. They do NOT say the data does not exist: "never published" and
"this page cannot see it" are different facts, one of them is false, and writing
the false one is the defect this project keeps catching.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
